From 02cc8a5107023906b56857fac8c5fcbcea3b830f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Farhan Riaz <its-forum@techlogix.com>
Date: Wed, 2 Sep 2026 15:58:27 +0500
Subject: [PATCH 05/11] core: Release loaded movie libraries by reachability,
 not by content root

The libraries of loaded movies were released once the display object the
movie was loaded into had been collected. That is the wrong condition:
content can keep a class out of a loaded SWF's ApplicationDomain, throw
the loaded content away, and instantiate the class later - which is how
AdventureQuest Worlds handles equipment - and the class then needs the
movie's characters, which were gone. Live testing of the game reproduced
this as "Tried to instantiate a non-registered character" and Error #2136
on every equipment load.

The right condition is the one Flash uses: a loaded SWF's definitions live
for as long as anything can still reach them - its ApplicationDomain, a
class, a method, a closure, an instance of one of its characters, or the
content itself - and not a moment longer. That is a question only the
collector can answer, because a library's own references to its movie and
its characters must not count, and they are what dominated the reference
counts before.

A movie library is now traced as an ephemeron. The root only traces its
anchors, all weak: the content root, the translation units of the movie's
ABC code (a class, method or script that is still reachable keeps its unit
alive), and - checked directly at the end of marking - the definition data
each character shares with its instances. Its contents are left untraced.
When marking has finished, `Library::resolve_releasable_libraries` asks
whether any anchor was reached; if so the contents are resurrected and
marking resumes from them, repeated to a fixpoint so that one library's
contents can keep another alive; the libraries nothing reached are dropped
and swept with everything else. The root movie's library and libraries
fetched by `ImportAssets`, which nothing traceable could anchor, are pinned.

`Player::collect_garbage` drives this using gc-arena's `MarkedArena` and
`Finalization`. `Player::collect_all_garbage` runs a full cycle for tests.

The weakly held class registry, the external-allocation accounting and the
`Loader.unloadAndStop` implementation are kept; the accounting is now
reported every frame rather than only when a library is released.

Dropping dead libraries does not use `PtrWeakKeyHashMap::retain`: that
implementation shifts the following entries back into the slot it has just
emptied and skips them, which here would leave a library holding pointers
to swept objects.

Adds a test that loads a SWF into a child domain, keeps a class from it,
unloads and drops the content, and instantiates the class later - which
has to find the class's SymbolClass-linked sprite and its shape - and then
releases the class. Two Rust-side tests check the library from the outside
at both points, after forced full collections: still resident while the
class is held, gone once it is released.
